# 🐧 Linux Commands Cheat Sheet

A practical, no-BS reference for daily Linux usage.  
Built while learning, debugging, and breaking things 🚀

---

## 📂 Navigation (Where am I?)
```bash
pwd              # shows your current location
ls               # list files in current folder
ls -la           # detailed list (including hidden files)
cd /path         # jump to a specific directory
cd ..            # go one level up
cd ~             # go back home
tree             # visualize folder structure (if installed)
```

---

## 📁 Files & Directories (Create / Move / Delete)
```bash
touch file.txt         # create a file or update timestamp
mkdir dir              # create a folder
mkdir -p a/b/c         # create nested folders in one go

cp src dst             # copy file/folder
cp -r dir1 dir2        # copy folder recursively

mv old new             # rename or move

rm file                # delete file
rm -rf dir             # ⚠️ force delete folder (dangerous)

rmdir emptydir         # remove empty folder only

stat file              # detailed file info
basename /path/file    # get filename
dirname /path/file     # get directory path
```

💡 *Rule: If you're unsure → don't use `rm -rf` blindly.*

---

## 📖 Viewing Files (Reading stuff quickly)
```bash
cat file           # dump full content
tac file           # reverse output (rare but cool)
head -n 20 file    # first 20 lines
tail -n 20 file    # last 20 lines
tail -f logfile    # live logs (very useful)

less file          # scroll + search (best option)
more file          # basic viewer

nl file            # show line numbers
wc -l file         # count lines
```

💡 *Use `less` instead of `cat` for large files.*

---

## 🔍 Search & Text Processing (Real power ⚡)
```bash
grep "pattern" file        # find text
grep -ri "pattern" dir/    # search everywhere (case-insensitive)

sort file                  # sort lines
uniq file                  # remove duplicates

cut -d ':' -f 1 file       # extract columns
awk '{print $1}' file      # print first column

sed -i 's/old/new/g' file  # replace text inside file

tr 'a-z' 'A-Z'             # lowercase → uppercase

paste file1 file2          # merge files line-wise
tee out.txt                # save + display output
```

💡 *`grep + awk + sed` = Linux superpower combo.*

